// Pipeline stages in a failure report.
//
// Every assertion here FAILS on purpose, and that is the whole point of the
// fixture: a pipeline's stages are collected while it runs and read only when
// something fails, so a program in which everything passes cannot observe them
// at all. The corpus is such a program — 583 cases, none of them failing — and
// that is why the block being dropped was invisible to the differential sweep
// while being wrong in ordinary user source.
//
// Two shapes, one per collector the interpreter switches on (eval.go:2964 and
// eval.go:5128), and they print differently from the same stage list:
//
//   - a pipe-valued BINDING, whose stages print under `defined as:` and are
//     printed UNCOMPACTED, every row carrying its whole prefix's source;
//   - a piped `testing.check` argument, whose stages ride on a `values:` row
//     for the whole subject and ARE compacted by the report.
//
// Both are here because a recorder that pre-compacted would be right through
// one and wrong through the other, and only the pair can tell.
//
// # What the third and fourth cases pin, and why they are shaped that way
//
// The compacted RENDERING is reachable from exactly two places, and neither is
// available to this file: `AssertionFailure.format`, and a `try` on a check
// inside a test body — which has a divergence of its own, unrelated to
// pipelines and present before any of this (the interpreter reports the
// check's own failure, the lowered body reports `test returned early`). So what is
// pinned here is the STAGE LIST rather than its compacted rendering: a pattern
// assertion whose `actual:` row inspects the whole AssertionFailure, which
// spells out `pipeline: [...]` with every stage's expression and value in
// order. Two independent recorders produce it and the comparison is byte for
// byte.
import {
    std/assertions.AssertionFailure
    std/testing
}

fn shout(s: String): String {
    String.to_upper(s)
}

fn piped_check(): Result<Bool, AssertionFailure> {
    found = try testing.check(
        "Ada Lovelace"
        |> String.contains?("Grace")
    )

    Ok(found)
}

fn unpiped_check(): Result<Bool, AssertionFailure> {
    found = try testing.check(String.contains?("Ada Lovelace", "Grace"))

    Ok(found)
}

test "a pipeline-bound name prints its stages uncompacted" {
    found =
        "Ada Lovelace"
        |> String.contains?("Grace")

    assert found
}

test "a three-stage binding prints one row per cumulative prefix" {
    shouted =
        "ada"
        |> shout()
        |> String.contains?("GRACE")

    assert shouted
}

test "a piped check carries its stages on the whole-subject row" {
    assert Ok(True) = piped_check()
}

test "an unpiped check beside it carries no pipeline at all" {
    assert Ok(True) = unpiped_check()
}
